<header class="nm-page-hero">
<p class="nm-page-label">Posta · Case study</p>
<h1>Building a podcast company before podcasting became obvious.</h1>
<p>Posta started in Buenos Aires in 2014. The work was to build more than a catalog: a product, a production system, marketing and distribution, audience and community relationships, a commercial model, and enough shared language for people and brands to understand a format that was still early.</p>
</header>
<section class="nm-page-main">
<div class="nm-two-col">
<h2>The context.</h2>
<div>
<p class="nm-page-copy">Podcasting had already proved that on-demand audio could produce new voices and formats. In Latin America, though, it was not yet an obvious category for listeners, advertisers, or media companies. There was no established playbook to import intact.</p>
<p class="nm-page-copy"> Posta had to make work people wanted to hear while helping create the market around the work.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="nm-two-col">
<h2>The problem.</h2>
<div>
<p class="nm-page-copy">How do you turn an emerging format into a durable media company before distribution, audience habits, production conventions, and brand demand have settled?</p>
<div class="nm-ledger">
<div class="nm-ledger-item"><span class="nm-page-label">Editorial</span><strong>Make shows worth returning to</strong><p>Original formats needed a recognizable point of view, reliable production, and a catalog rather than isolated experiments.</p></div>
<div class="nm-ledger-item"><span class="nm-page-label">Product</span><strong>Treat the company as a media product</strong><p>Programming, packaging, listening behavior, distribution, and audience feedback had to inform one another.</p></div>
<div class="nm-ledger-item"><span class="nm-page-label">Audience and community</span><strong>Create reasons to return, participate, and share</strong><p>Reach mattered, but so did repeat listening, direct feedback, recognizable formats, and relationships that could outlast a single release.</p></div>
<div class="nm-ledger-item"><span class="nm-page-label">Business</span><strong>Build demand around an unfamiliar format</strong><p>Branded audio and partnerships required translating podcasting into something organizations could understand and use without flattening the medium.</p></div>
<div class="nm-ledger-item"><span class="nm-page-label">Operations</span><strong>Make creative work repeatable</strong><p>Production, publishing, marketing, and client work needed workflows that could scale beyond the founders doing everything.</p></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="nm-two-col">
<h2>My role.</h2>
<div>
<p class="nm-page-copy">I co-founded Posta and worked across product, marketing, growth, positioning, and business development. Early company building rarely respects department boundaries. The useful job is often translating between the editorial idea, the audience behavior, the commercial opportunity, and the system the team can actually operate.</p>
<p class="nm-page-copy">The company built original programming, branded audio, distribution relationships, and work across multiple Latin American markets. Those outcomes belonged to a larger team and to my co-founders; this page describes the parts of the system I know from direct involvement.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="nm-two-col">
<h2>What changed.</h2>
<div>
<p class="nm-page-copy">Posta moved from an early format bet to an established Spanish-language podcast company. In December 2022, PodX acquired a majority stake in Posta, its first investment in Latin America. The company remains part of the PodX studio group.</p>
<div class="nm-contact-actions">
<p><strong>Public evidence:</strong> <a href="https://podx.com/studios/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PodX's studio directory</a>, the <a href="https://podnews.net/press-release/podx-group-posta" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">acquisition announcement</a>, and <a href="https://www.lanacion.com.ar/tecnologia/el-podcast-argentino-da-un-salto-internacional-con-la-asociacion-entre-posta-y-la-sueca-podx-nid07122022/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">LA NACION's 2022 report</a>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="nm-two-col">
<h2>What Posta taught me about AI-native media.</h2>
<div class="nm-ledger">
<div class="nm-ledger-item"><span class="nm-page-label">Distribution</span><strong>The business changes before the format feels settled</strong><p>Podcasting was not only radio delivered through a different file. On-demand distribution changed programming, measurement, packaging, and the relationship between a show and its audience.</p></div>
<div class="nm-ledger-item"><span class="nm-page-label">Economics</span><strong>Lower production costs change what gets to exist</strong><p>When a format becomes cheaper to make and distribute, more specific ideas become viable. AI will do this again, but cheaper output alone does not create a useful media product.</p></div>
<div class="nm-ledger-item"><span class="nm-page-label">Discovery</span><strong>Interfaces matter as much as content</strong><p>A strong show can remain invisible if the surrounding product cannot help the right listener find, understand, and return to it.</p></div>
<div class="nm-ledger-item"><span class="nm-page-label">Operations</span><strong>Workflows outlast tools</strong><p>Tools change quickly. A clear editorial standard, production sequence, ownership model, and recovery path are more durable.</p></div>
<p class="nm-page-copy">That is why I use <a href="ai-media.html">AI-native media</a> to mean a change in the operating system, not a layer of generated content added to an old one.</p>
</div>
</div>
